BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 692.00 Tm (Section 4: Firsthand Failure Modes, Traps & Economics Checks) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 669.77 Tm (The first sponsor-side trap is the stale contract. Gary Alton watched a multi-year deal freeze a client into) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 654.55 Tm ("stale discounts," with one exit penalty topping $500K, and in the same case study a carrier negotiated 75%) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 639.32 Tm (but passed along 65%, quietly keeping a 10% spread [Cxvmh46o824 @ 00:44:25]. Alton puts typical audit) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 624.10 Tm (findings at 10-30%, the size of leak a frozen contract can hide. Alan Pannier explains why the lock hurts) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 608.87 Tm (even without fraud: over the term the PBM keeps winning better retail pharmacy and manufacturer pricing,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 593.65 Tm (but the sponsor gets none of those gains because its pricing is fixed [ZuvAxUS0mII @ 00:01:02]. Justine at) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 578.42 Tm (TheCoyPharmer flags the trap inside the guarantee itself: PBM guarantees to plan sponsors are "typically a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 563.20 Tm (minimum level," so a sponsor promised AWP minus 89% that runs at minus 88% gets the shortfall paid) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 547.97 Tm ([H3p5Yw0Y-ek @ 00:07:20]. The catch is that the PBM runs separate MAC lists per guarantee, groups) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 532.75 Tm (sponsors into 5-8 cohorts, adjusts the lists daily or weekly, and may migrate an overperforming sponsor onto) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 517.52 Tm (a new MAC list [H3p5Yw0Y-ek @ 00:13:52]. The economics check is simple: without the monthly or weekly) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 502.30 Tm (reporting Justine tells resellers to demand, a sponsor cannot tell whether its contractual floor quietly became) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 487.07 Tm (its ceiling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 471.85 Tm (On rebates, the trap is believing the pass-through promise without seeing the plumbing. Frier Levitt) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 456.62 Tm (describes litigation that exposed PBM-owned rebate aggregators demanding, say, 25 cents on the dollar for) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 441.40 Tm (formulary access, keeping about 30% and sending 60-70 cents to the PBM, which then tells the plan "we're) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 426.17 Tm (going to turn it all over to you" [8mubYuuHvMI @ 00:01:20]. Frier Levitt adds that Medicare Part D and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 410.95 Tm (Medicare Advantage carry reporting duties while ERISA private plans have no such obligation, so the private) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 395.72 Tm (employer sees only what the contract forces [8mubYuuHvMI @ 00:02:16]. Benefits Blake puts a number on) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 380.50 Tm (the damage: only 59% of employers receive full traditional rebates and 57% full specialty rebates) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 365.27 Tm ([wUzyX8bEvt0 @ 00:11:06]. The second trap is the referee. Frier Levitt calls consultants paid by the PBM a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 350.05 Tm ("real conflict of interest," and Cora Opsahl's panel describes big PBMs paying consultants per RFP while) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 334.82 Tm (renewals get pitched as "only up 4% or 6%" [xHAyQelFkf8 @ 00:16:01]. Even the audit right gets hollowed) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 319.60 Tm (out with pre-approved auditors, 250-claim samples, no extrapolation and limited look-back, and the panel) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 304.37 Tm (notes a sponsor must hire an outside auditor because doing it in-house would violate the contract, even) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 289.15 Tm (though audits in that world find 30-40% overcharges.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 273.92 Tm (On the pharmacy side, the failure modes are cash-flow bleeds and audit compounding. A PA Pharmacists) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 258.70 Tm (speaker filled a drug whose cost jumped from $25 to $155 against a $55 MAC, a $100 loss on one fill, and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 243.47 Tm (lost at least $1,500 in two weeks because PBMs cut MAC prices fast but lag on increases [l81CzyzPR6M @) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 228.25 Tm (00:01:11]. Frier Levitt's team says the top audit discrepancy is inventory shortage, and some PBMs) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 213.02 Tm (reconcile at the NDC level, so buying a 50-count package against a 100-count billed NDC reads as a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 197.80 Tm (shortage and can trigger full recovery [67He43cmPRc @ 00:17:51]. Frier Levitt's New Jersey panel warns) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 182.57 Tm (that shrugging off a $5,000 finding burns pharmacies later: findings compound, as in $5,000 in 2021 and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 167.35 Tm ($25,000 in 2023 feeding a 2025 network review, so every finding gets appealed regardless of size) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 152.12 Tm ([hKqVi489zow @ 00:14:40]. Late fixes backfire too. Collecting house-account copays only after an audit) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 136.90 Tm (notice lands is a "red flag" [67He43cmPRc @ 00:28:44], and copay evidence produced after the audit starts) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 121.67 Tm (gets rejected. The economics check: a $10,000 audit you ignore is the down payment on a termination, and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 106.45 Tm (a MAC price you never appeal keeps bleeding margin until the list finally moves.) Tj ET
